{{also|déceptive}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*keh₂p-}}
From {{der|en|frm|déceptif}}, from {{der|en|la|dēceptīvus}}, from {{m|la|dēcipiō||I deceive}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈsɛp.tɪv/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-deceptive.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛptɪv|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Likely or [[attempting]] to [[deceive]].
#: {{syn|en|misleading|Thesaurus:deceptive}}
#: {{ux|en|'''deceptive''' practices}}
#: {{ux|en|Appearances can be '''deceptive'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1653|author={{w|John Bulwer}}|title=Anthropometamorphosis|location=London|publisher=William Hunt|section=Scene{{nbsp}}24|page=521|url=https://archive.org/details/anthropometamorp00jbjo/page/521|passage={{...}} others declare that no Creature can be made or transmuted into a better or worse, or transformed into another species {{...}} and ''Martinus Delrio'' the Jesuit accounts this degeneration of Man into a Beast to be an illusion, '''deceptive''' and repugnant to Nature;}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1789|translator={{w|Thomas Holcroft}}|title=The History of My Own Times|author={{w|Frederick the Great}}|location=London|publisher=G.G.J. and J. Robinson|section=Part 1, Chapter 12, p. 163|url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=hvd.hxj3at&view=1up&seq=451
|passage={{...}} at the opening of the campaign, the French, after various '''deceptive''' attempts on different places, suddenly invested Tournay.}}
#* '''1846''', {{w|Richard Chenevix Trench}}, ''Notes on the Miracles of Our Lord,'' London: John W. Parker, 2nd ed., 1847, Preliminary Essay, Chapter{{nbsp}}2, p.{{nbsp}}10,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/notesonmiracleslond00tren/page/10]</sup>
#*: language altogether '''deceptive''', and hiding the deeper reality from our eyes
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author={{w|Susan Sontag}}|title={{w|Illness as Metaphor}}|location=New York|publisher=Farrar, Straus and Giroux|chapter=2|page=13|url=https://archive.org/details/illnessasmetapho00susa
|passage={{...}} it is characteristic of [[tuberculosis|TB]] that many of its symptoms are '''deceptive'''—liveliness that comes from [[enervation]], rosy cheeks that look like a sign of health but come from fever—and an upsurge of vitality may be a sign of approaching death.}}

